---
title: Feature Extraction
mode: CQP
---

# Feature Extraction

## Initialization

Load helper macros and run `/init[...]`, which takes in one argument (the name of an s-
attribute) to define the subcorpus to query. The corresponding value for this s-attribute
is output alongside each span/hit count.

The macro creates a Named Query Result (NQR) with the name `Corpus`,
which should be re-activated after using a subquery.

```cqp
define macro < "macros/featex.m";
define macro < "macros/mwus.m";
/init_corpus[text_id];
```

The `/union[...]` and `/diff[...]` macros create a set union or difference of the provided
NQRs and save the result in the name of the first argument.

The `/feature[...]` and `feature_count[...]` macros take in a NQR and output a header with its name,
followed by a table of spans/hit counts for the s-attribute specified in `/init[...]`.

## Basic Counts

### Sentences

**Query**: s-attribute `s` (given by markup).

```cqp
Sentences = [] expand to s;
/feature_count[Sentences];
```

### Actual Words

**Query**: alphanumeric part-of-speech tags (excludes punctation).

```cqp
Words = [pos="\w+"];
/feature_count[Words];
```

### Content Words

**Query**: all nouns (N), verbs (V) excluding auxiliaries, adjectives (J), and adverbs (R).

```cqp
NVJRs = [pos="[NVJR].+" & pos!="RP|VMK?"];
VerbPhrases = (?longest) [pos="V[BDH][^I][RZ]?" | pos="VMK?"] [pos="XX"]? [pos="R.+"]* [pos="VBN"]? [pos="VBG"]? [:pos="V.[GN]K?":] within s;
VerbPhrases;
AuxiliaryVerbs = [pos!="XX|R.+"];
Corpus;
/diff[ContentWords, NVJRs, AuxiliaryVerbs];
/feature_count[ContentWords];
```

### Noun-based features

#### Common Nouns

**Definition**: include nouns that denote individual countable entities (count nouns) like 'book' as well as nouns that denote an undifferentiated mass or continuum (noncount nouns) like 'furniture' (Quirk et al. 2010, 246).

```cqp
CommonNouns = [pos="NN[ABLOTU]?[12]?|ND1"];
/feature_count[CommonNouns];
```

#### Proper Nouns

**Definition**: Proper nouns are names of specific people (Shakespeare), places (Milwaukee), months (September), days (Thursday), festivals (Christmas), magazines (Vogue), and so forth. They lack articles and are usually written with initial capital letters (Quirk et al. 2010, 288).

```cqp
ProperNouns = [pos="NP[DM]?[12]"];
/feature_count[ProperNouns];
```

#### Nominalizations

**Definition**: nouns created from adjectives or verbs through *derivation*. Derivation is based on affixation including prefixation and suffixation (Quirk et al. 2010, 1520).
- Prefixation: putting a prefix in front of the base, sometimes with, but more usually without, a change of word class
- Suffixation: putting a suffix after the base, sometimes without, but more usually with, a change of word class
**Query**: a positive wordlist of common nominalizing suffixes in combination with a negative wordlist to exclude certain high frequency false positives.

```cqp
define $NotNominalizedWords < "wordlists/nouns/NotNominalizedWords.txt";
define $NominalizingSuffixes < "wordlists/nouns/NominalizingSuffixes.txt";
Nominalizations = [pos="NN.+" & word=RE($NominalizingSuffixes)%c & word!=RE($NotNominalizedWords)%c];
/feature[Nominalizations];
```

#### Neoclassical Compounds

**Definition**: words containing a neoclassical prefix and/or neoclassical suffix. "Neoclassical" refers to items that are originally Greek or Latin (Quirk et al. 2010, 1545).
**Query**: a positive wordlist of common neoclassical affixes in combination with a negative wordlist to exclude certain high frequency false positives.

```cqp
define $NotNeoclassicalWords < "wordlists/nouns/NotNeoclassicalWords.txt";
define $NeoclassicalAffixes < "wordlists/nouns/NeoclassicalAffixes.txt";
NeoclassicalCompounds = [pos!="NP" & word=RE($NeoclassicalAffixes)%c & word!=RE($NotNeoclassicalWords)%c];
/feature[NeoclassicalCompounds];
```

### Pronouns

**Definition**: pronouns comprise a varied class of closed-class words with nominal function. By ‘nominal’ here we mean ‘noun-like’ or ‘like a noun phrase’. They may (A) substitute for some word or phrase, (B) signal, as personal pronouns do, that reference is being made to something which is given or known within the linguistic or situational context, or (C) stand for a very general concept, so that its reference includes the reference of untold more specific noun phrases (Quirk et al. 2010, 335).

```cqp
AllPronouns = [pos="A?P.+"];
/feature_count[AllPronouns];
```

#### Personal Pronouns

**Definition**: pronouns that have either a determinative function (my, our, etc.) or an independent function as a noun phrase (mine, ours, etc.) (Quirk et al. 2010, 361).

TODO: differentiate subjective and objective *you*/*it*

```cqp
# special case for lowercase `I`, which CLAWS consistently mistakes for ZZ1 and MC1
PronounI = [(pos="PPIS1" & word="I"%c) | word="I"%c];
PronounWe = [pos="PPIS2" & word="we"%c];

/feature_count[PronounI];
/feature_count[PronounWe];

PronounMe = [pos="PPIO1" & word="me"%c];
PronounUs = [pos="PPIO2" & word="us"%c];

/feature_count[PronounMe];
/feature_count[PronounUs];

PronounYou = [pos="PPY"];
/feature_count[PronounYou];

PronounHe = [pos="PPHS1" & word="he"%c];
PronounShe = [pos="PPHS1" & word="she"%c];
PronounIt = [word="it"%c];
PronounThey = [pos="PPHS2"];

/feature_count[PronounHe];
/feature_count[PronounShe];
/feature_count[PronounIt];
/feature_count[PronounThey];

PronounHim = [pos="PPHO1" & word="him"%c];
PronounHer = [pos="PPHO1" & word="her"%c];
PronounThem = [pos="PPHO2"];

/feature_count[PronounHim];
/feature_count[PronounHer];
/feature_count[PronounThem];

PronounIndefiniteNeutral = [pos="PN(\d1)?"];
/feature_count[PronounIndefiniteNeutral];

PronounIndefiniteSingular = [pos="PN1"];
/feature_count[PronounIndefiniteSingular];
```

#### Possessive Pronouns

**Definition**: pronouns that have either a determinative function (my, our, etc.) or an independent function as a noun phrase (mine, ours, etc.) (Quirk et al. 2010, 361)

TODO: *its* is never tagged as PPGE

```cqp
PronounMine = [pos="PPGE" & word="mine"%c];
PronounOurs = [pos="PPGE" & word="ours"%c];

/feature_count[PronounMine];
/feature_count[PronounOurs];

PronounYours = [pos="PPGE" & word="yours"%c];

/feature_count[PronounYours];

PronounHis = [pos="PPGE" & word="his"%c];
PronounHers = [pos="PPGE" & word="hers"%c];
PronounIts = [pos="PPGE" & word="its"%c];
PronounTheirs = [pos="PPGE" & word="theirs"%c];

/feature_count[PronounHis];
/feature_count[PronounHers];
/feature_count[PronounIts];
/feature_count[PronounTheirs];
```

#### Reflexive Pronouns

**Definition**: pronouns showing that the action in a sentence affects the person or thing that does the action (Longman Online Dictionary).

```cqp
PronounMyself = [pos="PPX1" & word="myself"%c];
PronounOurselves = [pos="PPX2" & word="ourselves"%c];

/feature_count[PronounMyself];
/feature_count[PronounOurselves];

PronounYourself = [pos="PPX1" & word="yourself"%c];
PronounYourselves = [pos="PPX2" & word="yourselves"%c];

/feature_count[PronounYourself];
/feature_count[PronounYourselves];

PronounHimself = [pos="PPX1" & word="himself"%c];
PronounHerself = [pos="PPX1" & word="herself"%c];
PronounItself = [pos="PPX1" & word="itself"%c];
PronounThemselves = [pos="PPX2" & word="themselves"%c];

/feature_count[PronounHimself];
/feature_count[PronounHerself];
/feature_count[PronounItself];
/feature_count[PronounThemselves];

PronounReflexiveIndefinite = [pos="PNX1(\d1)?"];
/feature_count[PronounReflexiveIndefinite];
```

### Determiners

**Definition**: closed-class items which occur before the noun acting as head of the noun phrase (or before its premodifiers) (Quirk et al. 2010, 253).

TODO: split these up further?

```cqp
Articles = [pos="AT1?"];
/feature_count[Articles];

WhDeterminers = [pos="DDQ(V|GE)"];
/feature_count[WhDeterminers];

PreDeterminers = [pos="DB2?"];
/feature_count[PreDeterminers];

PostDeterminers = [pos="DA[12RT]"];
/feature_count[PostDeterminers];

PossessiveDeterminers = [pos="APPGE"];
/feature_count[PossessiveDeterminers];

OtherDeterminers = [pos="DD[12]?" & word!="this|these|that|those"%c];
/feature_count[OtherDeterminers];
```

### Demonstratives

**Definition**: determiners of singular count nouns and singular noncount nouns ('this' and 'that') and determiners of plural count nouns ('these and those') (Quirk et al. 2010, 257).

**Query**:

1. **Demonstrative Determiners**: *this*, *these*, *that*, or *those* followed by a noun (N) or adjective (J)
2. **Demonstrative Pronouns**: all other instances of *this*, *these*, *that*, and *those*

```cqp
Demonstratives = [pos="DD[12]" & word="this|these|that|those"%c];
DemonstrativeDeterminers = [pos="DD[12]" & word="this|these|that|those"%c] np:[::] :: np -> np.pos = "[NJ].+" within s;
/feature[DemonstrativeDeterminers];

/diff[DemonstrativePronouns, Demonstratives, DemonstrativeDeterminers];
/feature[DemonstrativePronouns];
```

### Adjectives

Cases (Quirk et al. 2010: 418):

1. Attributive: "*useful* information"
2. Postpositive: "something *useful*"
3. Predicative: "This information is *useful*."

```cqp
AllAdjectives = [pos="J.+"];
/feature_count[AllAdjectives];
```

#### Attributive Adjectives

**Definition**: adjectives that "premodify the head of a noun phrase" (Quirk et al. 2010: 417).
**Query**: first extracts all premodifiers of nouns and then finds the adjectives within.

```cqp
AttributiveAdjectives = (?longest)(
    (
        ([word=","] | [word=","]? [pos="CCB?"])?
        [pos="R.+"]* [pos="J.+|M.+|N.+|"]
    )+
    [pos="N.+"]+
) within s;
AttributiveAdjectives;
AttributiveAdjectives = [pos="J.+|M.+"];
/feature[AttributiveAdjectives];
Corpus; # reactivate corpus
```

#### Postpositive Adjectives

**Definition**: adjectives that "immediately follow the noun or pronoun they modify" (Quirk et al. 2010: 418).
**Query**:

1. any compound singular indefinite pronoun (PN1) ending in *-body*, *-one*, or *-thing* or a locative adverb (RL) ending in *-where*, followed by an adjective (J)
e.g., "something *nice*"

1. adjectives ending in *-able* or *-ible* when a noun is modified by a superlative adjecitve, the word *only*, or an ordinal
e.g., "the best use *possible*"

1. adjectives following a noun with a prepositional phrase or *to*-infinitive as complement
e.g., "box *full* of chocolate"

1. *proper* in the sense of "strictly defined"
e.g., "the City of London *proper*"

1. institutionalized expressions (mostly in official designations)
e.g., "president _elect" and "heir *apparent*"

TODO: add other cases listed in Quirk et al. (2010)

```cqp
PostpositiveAdjectives1 = [(word=".+(body|one|thing)"%c & pos="PN1") | (word=".+where" & pos="RL")] [pos="J.+"] within s;
set PostpositiveAdjectives1 match matchend;
PostpositiveAdjectives2 = [word="only" | pos="JJT" | pos="MD"] [pos="N.+"] [word=".+[ai]ble" & pos="J.+"] within s;
set PostpositiveAdjectives2 match matchend;
PostpositiveAdjectives3 = [pos="N.+"] [pos="R.+"]* @[pos="J.+"] [pos="I[FIOW]" | pos="TO"] within s;
set PostpositiveAdjectives3 match target;
PostpositiveAdjectives4 = [pos="N.+"] [word="proper"%c] within s;
set PostpositiveAdjectives4 match matchend;
PostpositiveAdjectives5 = "president"%c "elect"%c | "heir"%c "apparent"%c;
set PostpositiveAdjectives5 match matchend;

/union[
    PostpositiveAdjectives,
    PostpositiveAdjectives1,
    PostpositiveAdjectives2,
    PostpositiveAdjectives3,
    PostpositiveAdjectives4,
    PostpositiveAdjectives5
];
/feature[PostpositiveAdjectives];
```

#### Predicative Adjectives

**Definition**: adjectives that "function as subject complement or object complement" (Quirk et al. 2010: 417).
**Query**: negative query (all adjectives that are not attributive or postpositive).

```cqp
/diff[PredicativeAdjectives, AllAdjectives, AttributiveAdjectives, PostpositiveAdjectives];
/feature[PredicativeAdjectives];
```

### Prepositions

**Definition**: a preposition expresses a relation between two entities, one being that represented by the prepositional complement, the other by another part of the sentence. The prepositional complement is characteristically a noun phrase, a nominal wh-clause, or a nominal -ing clause (Quirk et al. 2010, 657).
**Query**: based on part-of-speech

```cqp
Prepositions = [pos="I.+"];
/feature_count[Prepositions];
```

### Verbs

TODO: extract verb phrases and then check for features below?

```cqp
AllVerbs = [pos="V.+"];
/feature_count[AllVerbs];
```

#### Finite Verbs

**Definition**: verbs that express either tense or modality.
**Query**: based on part-of-speech

For normalization of other verb features.

```cqp
FiniteVerbs = [pos="V.[^ING].?" | pos="VMK?"];
/feature[FiniteVerbs];
```

#### Past Verbs

**Definition**: verbs that occur as a finite form in the past tense (Quirk et al. 2010, 97).
**Query**: past tense verbs plus *used to*

- also counts *used to* not tagged as a modal when preceded by a noun or pronoun
- includes *I* explicitly because CLAWS tags it incorrectly when not capitalized

```cqp
PastVerbs = [pos="V.D.?" | (pos="VMK" & word="used"%c)] | [pos="[NP].+" | word="I"%c] "used"%c "to"%c within s;
/feature[PastVerbs];
```

#### Future Constructions

**Definition**: future time is rendered by means of modal auxiliaries, semi-auxiliaries, or by the simple present or present progressive forms (Quirk et al. 2010, 213).
**Query**:

- will: a form of *will* as a verb followed by an infinitive with optional negation and adverbs in-between
- going-to:
    1. a form of *be* followed by optional negation and adverbs, the phrase *going to*, and an infinitive
    2. the contractions *gonna* and *I'mma* (plus variations) and an infinitive
- about-to: a form of *be* followed by optional negation and adverbs, the phrase *about to*, and an infinitive

TODO: is-to futures? (may be too ambiguous)

```cqp
# use V.+ instead of VM to counteract tagging errors (hopfully unproblematic
# since the combination of tag, surface form, and infinitive should be unambiguous)
WillFutures = [pos="V.+" & word="will|'ll|wo|shall|sha"%c] [pos="XX"]? [pos="RR.*"]* [pos="V.I"] within s;
/feature[WillFutures];

GoingToFutures1 = [pos="VB.+"] [pos="XX"]? [pos="RR.*"]* [word="going" | pos="VVGK"] [pos="TO"] [pos="V.I"] within s;
GoingToFutures2 = [word="I'?mm?a"%c | word="gonna"%c] [pos="V.I"];
/union[GoingToFutures, GoingToFutures1, GoingToFutures2];
/feature[GoingToFutures];

AboutToFutures = [pos="VB.+"] [pos="XX"]? [pos="RR.*"]* "about"%c [pos="TO"] [pos="V.I"] within s;
/feature[AboutToFutures];
```

### Modal Verbs

**Definition**: verbs that express permission, obligation, and volition as well as possibility, necessity, and prediction (Quirk et al. 2010, 219). 
**Query**: *can*, *would*, *could*, *should*, *may*, *must*, *might* (plus contractions and excluding will)

TODO: have to, be able to, be allowed to, etc.

```cqp
ModalVerbs = [pos="VM" & word!="will|'ll|wo|shall|sha"%c];
/feature_count[ModalVerbs];
```

## Grammatical Structures

### To-Infinitives

**Definition**: constructions in which the infinitive marker 'to' is followed by the infinitive form of a verb (Quirk et al. 2010, 97).
**Query**: the word *to* followed by an infinitive (with optional adverbs in-between).

```cqp
ToInfinitives = [pos="TO" | word="to" %c] [pos="RR.*"]* [pos="V.I"] within s;
set ToInfinitives match matchend;
/feature[ToInfinitives];
```

### Passives

**Definition**: a form of the verb 'be' (or 'get' in informal contexts) followed by a past participle (optionally followed by a by-phrase introducing the agent) (Quirk et al. 2010, 159).

TODO: disambiguate predicative adjectives
TODO: bare (i.e. verbless) passives
TODO: passive interrogatives
TODO: improve nonfinite cases

```cqp
# be + (not + adverbs)? + being? + past participle
# have + (not + adverbs)? + been + being? + past participle
NonModalPassives = (
    [pos="VB[^N].*"] [pos="XX"]? [pos="R.*"]* |
    [pos="VH.+"] [pos="XX"]? [pos="R.*"]* [pos="VBN"] [pos="R.*"]*
) [pos="VBG"]? [pos="V.N.?"] within s;
set NonModalPassives match matchend;

# modal + (not + adverbs)? be (being)? + past participle
# modal + (not + adverbs)? have been (being)? + past participle
ModalPassives = (
    [pos="VM"] [pos="XX"]? [pos="R.*"]*
    ([pos="VBI"] [pos="R.*"]* | [pos="VH0"] [pos="R.*"]* [pos="VBN"])
    [pos="VBG"]? [pos="V.N.?"]
) within s;
set ModalPassives match matchend;

# to + be + past participle
ToPassives = [pos="TO"] [pos="VBI"] [pos="V.N.?"] within s;
set ToPassives match matchend;

# get + past participle
GetPassives = [word="get(s|ting)?|got(ten)?"] ([pos="AT1?"]? [pos="N.+"] | [pos="P.+"])? [pos="R.*"]* [pos="V.N.?"] within s;
set GetPassives match matchend;

/union[
    Passives,
    NonModalPassives,
    ModalPassives,
    ToPassives,
    GetPassives
];
/feature[Passives];
```

TODO: perfect and progressive aspect

### Coordination and Subordination

**Definition**:
- coordination: according to the principle of indefinite extensibility, two or more units of the same status on the grammatical hierarchy may constitute a single unit of the same kind. It is typically signalled by a link word, so-called coordinating conjunction (Quirk et al. 2010, 46).
- subordination: one clause is made a constituent of another clause, i.e. clauses which are embedded in other clauses. Subordinate clauses are often introduced by a subordinating conjunction (Quirk et al. 2010, 44).

```cqp
CoordinatingConjunctions = [pos="CCB?"];
/feature_count[CoordinatingConjunctions];

SubordinatingConjunctions = [pos="CS[ANTW]?"];
/feature_count[SubordinatingConjunctions];
```

### Interrogatives

**Definition**: questions which are formally marked in one of to ways: (I) yes-no interrogatives: the operator is placed in front of the subject or (II) wh-interrogatives: the interrogative wh-element is positioned initially (Quirk et al. 2010, 803).
**Query**:

1. question marks
2. wh-questions
3. yes-no questions
4. tag questions

TODO: clauses before interrogatives?

```cqp
QuestionMarks = "\?" expand to s;
WhQuestions = <s> [pos="CCB?" | word="\p{P}"]? [pos="..Q.*"] [pos="V.*"] within s expand to s;
# AT1 has many false positives
# CLAWS tags many "is that ..." as CST
YNQuestions = <s> [(pos="CCB?"  & word!="nor"%c) | word="\p{P}"]? [pos="NP[12]|UH"]? [pos="VH[0Z]" | pos="VB[DMRZ][RZ]?" | pos="VD[0DZ]" | pos="VM"] [pos="XX"]? [pos="PPY" | pos="PP[HI].+" | pos="D[BD]1?" | pos="AT" | pos="NN.?" | pos="APPGE" | pos="CST" | pos="EX"] within s expand to s;
TagQuestions = [pos="V[BDH][^GIN][RZ]?" | pos="VM"] [pos="XX" & word="n't"] [pos="PPHS[1S][12]?"] </s> within s expand to s;

/union[Interrogatives, QuestionMarks, WhQuestions, YNQuestions, TagQuestions];
/feature[Interrogatives];
```

### Imperatives

**Definition**: sentences which normally have no subject, but either a main verb in the base form or (less commonly) an auxiliary in the base form followed by the appropriate form of the main verb (Quirk et al. 2010, 827). 
**Query**:

1. positive imperatives
e.g., "give me the remote"
2. negative imperatives
e.g., "don't give in"
excludes mental verbs (e.g., know, want) to catch subject ellipses
3. let imperatives
e.g., "let's go to the beach"

TODO: better disambiguation
TODO: more clauses before imperatives
TODO: cases like "Somebody watch my dog."

```cqp
define $MentalWords < "wordlists/process_types/MentalWords.txt";

PositiveImperatives = <s> ([pos="TO"] []+ ",")? [pos="CCB?" | (pos="R.+" & pos!="RRQV?") | word="\p{P}"]? [pos="V.0"] [pos!="V.Z.*|VM.*|PP.S.?|PPY"] []* [word!="\?"]</s>;
NegativeImperatives = <s> ([pos="TO"] []+ ",")? [pos="CCB?" | word="\p{P}"]? [pos="VD0" & word!=RE($MentalWords)%c] [pos="XX"] [pos="V.I"] [: pos!="RRQ" :] within s expand to s;

# periphrastic first and third person imperatives with let
LetImperatives = [pos="VM21"] [pos="VM22"] [pos="RR.*"]* [pos="V.I.?"] within s expand to s;

/union[Imperatives, PositiveImperatives, NegativeImperatives, LetImperatives];
/feature[Imperatives];
```

### Titles

**Definition**: words ascribing role, task, or status.
**Query**:

1. titles following a noun (e.g., M.A.)
2. titles preceding a noun (e.g., Dr.)
3. wordlist to improve recall due to possible tagging errors
4. fixed expressions (at the moment only "the honourable...")

```cqp
NNAs = [pos="NNA"];
NNBs = [pos="NNB"];
define $TitleWords < "wordlists/TitleWords.txt";
TitleWords = @[word=RE($TitleWords)%c] [pos="NP.+"] within s;
set TitleWords matchend target;
Honorables = (?longest) "the"%c "honou?rable"%c [pos="[JN].+"]+ within s;
FixedExpressions = "your"%c "honors?"%c | "my"%c "lords?|lad(y|ies)"%c;
/union[Titles, NNAs, NNBs, TitleWords, Honorables, FixedExpressions];
/feature[Titles];
```

### Greetings

**Definition**: polite expressions usually occurring at the start and end of an interaction.

#### Salutations

**Query**:

1. casual salutations
2. variations of "good ..."
3. variations of "good ..." with addressee
4. generic addressee singular
5. generic addressee plural
6. royal address
7. variations of "dear ..."

```cqp
CasualSalutations = "hello|hi(ya)?|heya?|yo|sup|whazzup|g'day";

GoodSalutations1 = <s> "good"%c "day|morning|afternoon|evening"%c </s>;
GoodSalutations2 = "good"%c "day|morning|afternoon|evening"%c ("to"%c "you"%c | [pos="N.+"]+) within s;

GenericSalutations1 = TAB "to"%c "whom"%c "it"%c "may"%c "concern"%c within s;
GenericSalutations2 = TAB "lad(y|ies)"%c [pos="CCB?"] "gentlem[ae]n"%c within s;
RoyalSalutations = TAB "your"%c "royal"%c? "majesty|highness|excellence"%c within s;

DearSalutations = (?longest)(
    ([pos!="APPGE|AT1?" & word!=","] | <s>)
    [word="dear"%c] ([pos="NNB"] "\."?)?
    [pos="N.+|DB2?" & pos!="NN[UT][12]?" & word!="god|lord"%c]+
    ([pos="CC"] ([pos="NNB"] "\."?)? [pos="N.+|DB2?"]+)*
) within s;

/union[
    Salutations,
    CasualSalutations,
    GoodSalutations1,
    GoodSalutations2,
    GenericSalutations1,
    GenericSalutations2,
    RoyalSalutations,
    DearSalutations
];
```

#### Valedictions

**Query**:

1. unambiguous expressions (e.g., "best wishes")
2. ambiguous expressions with comma (e.g., "best,")

TODO: what about "X sends best wishes"
TODO: count coordinated expressions separately?

```cqp
Valedictions1 = (
    (<s> | [pos!="APPGE" & word!="your|their"]) [pos="IW"]?
    "personal|best|(warm|kind(est)?)"%c "regards|wishes"%c |
    "yours"%c "sincerely|faithfully|truly"%c |
    [pos="RG"]? "sincerely|truly|respectfully|cordially"%c "yours"%c
);

Valedictions2 = <s> "best|cheers|yours|cordially|respectfully|warmly|regards|sincerely|thanks"%c ",";
/union[Valedictions, Valedictions1, Valedictions2];
```

```cqp
/union[Greetings, Salutations, Valedictions];
/feature[Greetings];
```

### Adverbs

**Definition**: the adverb functions as the head of an adverb phrase and can take the syntactic function of an adverbial or premodifier of adjective and adverb (Quirk et al. 2010, 439-440). 

TODO: handle phrasal verbs (e.g., "throw away")

```cqp
define $SpaceWords < "wordlists/adverbs/SpaceWords.txt";
RLs = [: word!="look(s|ing|ed)?"%c :] [pos="RL(\d1)?" & word!="together|downstream" %c] within s;
SpaceWords = [pos="R[^P].+" & word=RE($SpaceWords)%c];

/union[SpaceAdverbs, RLs, SpaceWords];
/feature_count[SpaceAdverbs];

define $TimeWords < "wordlists/adverbs/TimeWords.txt";
RTs = [pos="RT(\d1)?"]; # only count ditto tags once
TimeWords = [pos="R[^P].+" & word=RE($TimeWords)%c];
TimeMetaphors = [word="look(s|ing|ed)?" %c] [word="forward|back(ward)?" %c] within s;
set TimeMetaphors match matchend;

/union[TimeAdverbs, RTs, TimeWords, TimeMetaphors];
/feature_count[TimeAdverbs];
```

## Themes

The system of Theme belongs to the textual metafunction of the language. It is concerned with the organization of information within individual clauses and, through this, with the organization of the larger text (Martin, Matthiessen, and Painter 1997, 21). Theme refers to the anchor of the clause and is (in English) located at the beginning of the clause whereas rheme refers to the rest of the clause (Bartlett 2014, 132). Technically, thematic elements are located in sentence-initial position and indicate the local context of clause as a piece of text. Incomplete sentences, e.g., headings, are not annotated for theme, since these sentences contain only rheme (Halliday and Matthiessen 2014, chap. 4.6.2).

TODO: consider multiple themes?

### Textual

#### Continuatives

**Definition**: located in sentence-initial position and indicate a relationship to previous discourse (Martin, Matthiessen, and Painter 1997, 26).

```cqp
InitialContinuatives = <s> [word="\p{P}"]? ([pos="UH" | word="yes|well|oh|now|please"%c] | "no"%c [: word!="matter"%c & pos!="[J|N].+" :]) within s;
/feature[InitialContinuatives];
```

#### Conjunctions

**Definition**: located in sentence-initial position and link two clauses in a coordinating relation or mark one clause as dependent on another (Martin, Matthiessen, and Painter 1997, 25).

```cqp
define $ParatacticWords = "for and nor but or yet either neither"; # fanboys plus (n)either
InitialCoordinators = <s> ([word="\p{P}"]? ([pos="CCB?" | word=RE($ParatacticWords)%c | (word="for"%c & pos="CS")] | [word="so"%c] [: pos!="JJ" :])) within s;
InitialSubordinators = <s> [word="\p{P}"]? [pos="CS[ANTW]?" & word!="that"%c] within s;
/union[InitialConjunctions, InitialCoordinators, InitialSubordinators];
/feature[InitialConjunctions];
```

##### Conjunctive Adjuncts

**Definition**: located in sentence-initial position and provide a cohesive link back to previous discourse (Martin, Matthiessen, and Painter 1997, 25).

Halliday & Matthiessen (2014: Table 3-4)

```cqp
define $ConjunctiveAdjuncts < "wordlists/adjuncts/ConjunctiveAdjuncts.txt";
InitialConjunctiveAdjunctWords = <s> [word="\p{P}"]? [word=RE($ConjunctiveAdjuncts)%c] within s;
InitialConjunctiveAdjunctMWUs = <s> [word="\p{P}"]? /mwu_conjunctiveadjuncts[] within s;
/union[InitialConjunctiveAdjuncts, InitialConjunctiveAdjunctWords, InitialConjunctiveAdjunctMWUs];
/feature[InitialConjunctiveAdjuncts];
```

##### Ordinal Numbers

**Definition**: text-structuring enumerations in sentence-initial position (Martin, Matthiessen, and Painter 1997, 25).

```cqp
InitialNumbers = <s> [word="\p{P}"]? [word="\d+"] [word="\p{P}"] within s;
/feature[InitialNumbers];
```

### Interpersonal

#### Modal Adjuncts

**Definition**: typically realized by an adverb in sentence-initial position; provide the speaker’s comment, assessment or attitude towards the message (adverbs expressing modality or stance) (Martin, Matthiessen, and Painter 1997, 25).

Halliday & Matthiessen (2014: Table 3-5)

```cqp
define $ModalAdjuncts < "wordlists/adjuncts/ModalAdjuncts.txt";
InitialModalAdjunctWords = <s> [word="\p{P}"]? [pos="XX"]? [word=RE($ModalAdjuncts)%c] within s;
InitialModalAdjunctMWUs = <s> [word="\p{P}"]? /mwu_modaladjuncts[] within s;
/union[InitialModalAdjuncts, InitialModalAdjunctWords, InitialModalAdjunctMWUs];
/feature[InitialModalAdjuncts];
```

#### Finite Verbs

**Definition**: finite forms of the verb occurring in the sentence-initial position (Martin, Matthiessen, and Painter 1997, 25).

TODO: separate positive imperatives (experiential) from negatives imperatives and yes-no questions (interpersonal)

```cqp
InitialFiniteVerbs = <s> [pos="V.[^ING].?"  | pos="VMK?"] within s;
/feature[InitialFiniteVerbs];
```

### Other

#### Nonfinite Clauses

**Definition**: a clause whose verb element is non-finite. They lack tense markers and modal auxiliaries and frequently lack a subject and a subordinating conjunction (Quirk et al. 2010, 995).

TODO: disambiguate experiential, interpersonal, and textual uses

```cqp
InitialToInfinitives = <s> [word="\p{P}"]? [pos="TO" | word="to"%c] [pos="R.+"]* [pos="V.I"] within s expand to s;
InitialParticiples = <s> [word="\p{P}"]? [pos="V.[GN].?"] within s expand to s;
/union[InitialNonFinites, InitialToInfinitives, InitialParticiples];
/feature[InitialNonFinites];
```

## Transitivity

Transitivity nelongs to the experiential metafunction and is the overall grammatical resource for construing goings on (Martin, Matthiessen, and Painter 1997, 100). Transitivity encompasses the grammatical system by which we exchange information at the clause level. Central elements that are being taken into account in transitivity analysis are process, participants, and circumstances.

TODO: improve wordlists

### Existential Processes

**Definition**: resemble relational processes in that they construe a participant involved in a process of being. Prototypically, the Existent is introduced with 'There' followed by a form of be (Martin, Matthiessen and Painter 1997, 109). 

Halliday & Matthiessen (2014: Table 5-26)

TODO: metereological processes (considered kind of borderline)?

Uses some matching hacks for extracting the verb instead of *there*.

```cqp
define $ExistentialWords < "wordlists/process_types/ExistentialWords.txt";
ExistentialWords = TAB [word="there"%c] * [word=RE($ExistentialWords)%c] within s;
set ExistentialWords match matchend;

ExistentialPos = TAB [pos="EX"] * [pos="V.+"] within s;
set ExistentialPos match matchend;
ExistentialTheres = "there"%c (@[pos="V[BDH].+"] | (@[pos="VM"] | @[pos="VMK"] [pos="XX"]? [pos="TO"]?)) within s;
set ExistentialTheres match target;
set ExistentialTheres matchend target;
/union[ExistentialProcesses, ExistentialWords, ExistentialPos, ExistentialTheres];
/feature[ExistentialProcesses];
```

### Relational Processes

**Definition**: construe being in two different modes – attribution and identification (Martin, Matthiessen and Painter 1997, 106).

Halliday & Matthiessen (2014: Table 5-20, 5-21)

```cqp
# MU query with right constraint to avoid hits for cases like "was giving"
# Ideally, we would disallow any other verbs until the next clause.
BeHaveProcesses = MU(meet [pos="V[BH].+"] not [pos="V.[NG]K?" | pos="JK"] 1 3);

define $RelationalWords < "wordlists/process_types/RelationalWords.txt";
RelationalWords = [pos="V.+" & word=RE($RelationalWords)%c];

/union[RelationalProcesses, BeHaveProcesses, RelationalWords];
# filter processes already identified earlier
/diff[RelationalProcesses, RelationalProcesses, ExistentialProcesses];
/feature[RelationalProcesses];
```

### Verbal Processes

**Definition**: represent processes of ‘saying’, including the different modes of saying (asking, commanding, offering, stating) as well as semiotic processes that are not necessarily verbal (showing, indicating) (Martin, Matthiessen and Painter 1997, 108).

Halliday & Matthiessen (2014: 5-25)

TODO: add query for *be like*

```cqp
define $VerbalWords < "wordlists/process_types/VerbalWords.txt";
VerbalProcesses = [pos="V.+" & word=RE($VerbalWords)];
/diff[VerbalProcesses, VerbalProcesses, RelationalProcesses, ExistentialProcesses];
/feature[VerbalProcesses];
```

### Mental Processes

**Definition**: construe a person involved in conscious processing, including processes of perception, cognition and affection (Martin, Matthiessen and Painter 1997, 105).

Halliday & Matthiessen (2014: Table 5-10)

```cqp
MentalProcesses = [pos="V.+" & word=RE($MentalWords)];
/diff[MentalProcesses, MentalProcesses, VerbalProcesses, RelationalProcesses, ExistentialProcesses];
/feature[MentalProcesses];
```

### Material Processes

**Definition**: processes that construe doings and happenings (Martin, Matthiesen and Painter 1997, 103).

Halliday & Matthiessen (2014: Table 5-5)

```cqp
define $MaterialWords < "wordlists/process_types/MaterialWords.txt";
MaterialProcesses = [pos="V.+" & word=RE($MaterialWords)];
/diff[MaterialProcesses, MaterialProcesses, MentalProcesses, VerbalProcesses, RelationalProcesses, ExistentialProcesses]
/feature[MaterialProcesses];
```
